## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design a practical streaming PCA workflow suitable for beginners, focusing on:
- Online dimensionality reduction techniques
- Missing data handling in streaming contexts
- Component selection strategies
- Translating statistical outputs into actionable alerts
- Meeting strict latency requirements

## Key Concepts

- **IncrementalPCA**: Ideal for streaming data with mini-batch updates, allowing real-time adaptation
- **Missing Data Imputation**: Simple mean imputation maintains speed while handling occasional gaps
- **Component Selection**: Explained variance threshold (>90%) provides automated dimensionality decision
- **Loadings Interpretation**: High-loading features drive deterministic alert generation
- **Latency Optimization**: Efficient batch processing ensures sub-5ms transaction processing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simplified online PCA implementation
// Initialize IncrementalPCA with 6 components
const pca = new IncrementalPCA(n_components: 6, batch_size: 1000);

// Process streaming transactions
function processTransaction(transaction) {
  // Impute missing values with feature means
  const cleanData = imputeMissing(transaction, featureMeans);

  // Project to lower dimension
  const components = pca.partial_fit(cleanData);

  // Generate alerts from top loadings
  return generateAlerts(components, loadings);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of online PCA under real-time constraints, drift, and evolving feature spaces across distributed centers.

## Key Concepts
- Online PCA with fixed basis; drift detection on loadings; streaming imputation; feature rollout handling; fairness constraints; latency budgeting.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch for online PCA update
function onlinePCAUpdate(x, state) {
  // x: 120 features, may contain missing
  const z = imputeAndScale(x, state.scaler);
  // Update components via incremental SVD / Oja rule
  state.components = updateBasis(state.components, z, state.learningRate);
  // Update loadings and detect drift
  return state;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection sensitivity across data-center cohorts?
- What would you change if a new feature is introduced mid-stream?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an online, drift-aware PCA system that remains interpretable and compliant under strict latency constraints typical of autonomous vehicle systems.

## Key Concepts
- Online incremental PCA (IPCA) with forgetting factors
- Concept drift detection and adaptation strategies
- Dynamic feature addition/removal via partial-fit methods
- Loading stability tracking for interpretability preservation
- Millisecond-latency optimization through parallel computation
- Governance-aligned explainability through loading-to-alert mappings

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: partial_fit on new data, adjust window, recompute top loadings
function updateIPCA(newData, currentModel, windowSize, forgettingFactor) {
  // Partial fit with forgetting factor
  const updatedModel = partialFit(newData, currentModel, forgettingFactor);
  // Adjust moving window
  const windowedModel = adjustWindow(updatedModel, windowSize);
  // Recpute interpretable loadings
  return computeTopLoadings(windowedModel);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you set drift-detection thresholds to balance automatic fallback versus adaptation?
- What validation approach would you use comparing synthetic drift scenarios against real-world telemetry patterns?
- How do you ensure loading stability while maintaining computational efficiency?

